.TH CTI-AUDIT 1 "2026-11-19" "cti-agent" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
cti-audit \- write to the fleet's audit trail, prune it, or check it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cti-audit
.B \-\-action
.I ACTION
.B \-\-outcome
.IR success | failure | refused
.RB [ \-\-target
.IR TARGET ]
.RB [ \-\-detail
.IR TEXT ]
.RB [ \-\-lane
.IR NAME ]
.PP
.B cti-audit \-\-prune \-\-days
.I N
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.PP
.B cti-audit \-\-check
.SH DESCRIPTION
The fleet keeps an audit trail of every security-relevant action it takes: a
mail sent, a ticket filed, mail moved, an owner changed. Each event says what
was done, to what, by whom, when, and whether it worked.
.PP
The Go commands write their events themselves, at the Graph and Jira clients,
so a new command that sends mail or edits a ticket is covered without
remembering to.
.B cti-audit
is the same writer for scripts
.RB ( fleet\-owners (1)
uses it), plus the retention rule and a check.
.SS The trail
RFC 5424 syslog lines, one per event, in
.B /var/log/cti\-agent/audit\-YYYY\-MM\-DD.log
(one file per UTC day; the directory is
.B AUDIT_DIR
and systemd creates it for the service account). RFC 5424 is what rsyslog,
syslog-ng, Splunk, Elastic, Wazuh, Azure Monitor and every SIEM read, so a
collector can tail the files, or the fleet can send the same lines on
(see FORWARDING). Example, wrapped:
.PP
.nf
<86>1 2026-11-19T16:04:05.123Z host-a cti\-jira 4242 jira.issue.create
  [audit@32473 v="1" lane="cti\-jira" actor="ctiagent" run="b3f2..."
  target="CR\-100" outcome="success"] type=Task
.fi
.PP
The priority is facility authpriv with severity info for a success, warning
for a failure and notice for a refusal. The message id is the action. The
.B run
is systemd's invocation id (or
.BR FLEET_RUN_ID ),
so every line one unit run wrote shares it. Values are sanitised (no control
characters, capped, and the three characters RFC 5424 escapes are escaped), so
a hostile subject or recipient cannot forge a line. No message bodies and no
credentials are ever written, and a
.B \-\-dry\-run
writes nothing because nothing happened.
.PP
If the directory cannot be written, the event goes to stderr, which every unit
sends to the journal, and one warning says why: a missing directory never
stops a lane, and an event is not silently lost.
.SS What the trail does not prove
The writer is the same service account that runs the lanes, so a lane
compromised as that account could alter the local files. The files are a
record, not tamper-proof evidence. Shipping them off the host is what makes
them evidence.
.SS Forwarding
Off unless
.B AUDIT_SYSLOG_ADDR
(host:port) is set; nothing in the fleet knows which product is on the other
end.
.B AUDIT_SYSLOG_PROTO
is
.B tls
(the default: certificate and host name verified, TLS 1.2 or better),
.BR tcp ,
or
.BR udp ;
a plaintext protocol has to be asked for by name. TLS and TCP use RFC 6587
octet counting. A collector that is down costs one warning per run and
nothing else; the local file still has the event. The other way to ship the
trail needs no setting here: point the collector's file reader at
.BR audit\-*.log .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-action " ACTION"
Write one event. ACTION is what was done, such as
.BR owners.set ;
it becomes the syslog message id.
.TP
.BI \-\-outcome " OUTCOME"
With
.BR \-\-action :
.BR success ,
.B failure
or
.BR refused .
.TP
.BI \-\-target " TARGET"
With
.BR \-\-action :
what it was done to.
.TP
.BI \-\-detail " TEXT"
With
.BR \-\-action :
a short safe note. Never a body or a secret.
.TP
.BI \-\-lane " NAME"
With
.BR \-\-action :
the lane or command name (default cti-audit).
.TP
.B \-\-prune
Delete audit files older than
.BR \-\-days .
Only regular files named
.B audit\-DATE.log
in the audit directory are touched; a symlink, a directory or any other name
is left alone.
.TP
.BI \-\-days " N"
With
.BR \-\-prune :
how many days to keep, 1 or more. The mailbox cleanup lane passes
.B AUDIT_RETAIN_DAYS
(default 120).
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
With
.BR \-\-prune :
list what would be deleted and delete nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Report whether the trail directory is writable, and whether the collector, if
one is configured, is reachable. Exit 1 if either is not.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AUDIT_DIR
Where the trail is kept (default /var/log/cti\-agent).
.B off
discards events, for tests and development machines only.
.TP
.B AUDIT_RETAIN_DAYS
How long the cleanup keeps files (default 120).
.TP
.B AUDIT_SYSLOG_ADDR
host:port of a syslog collector. Unset means no forwarding.
.TP
.B AUDIT_SYSLOG_PROTO
tls (default), tcp or udp.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, including when a write fell back to stderr (a script mid-task
must not fail because the log is unwritable). 1 when
.B \-\-check
finds a problem or a prune fails. 2 for a usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Is the trail writable, and is the collector reachable:
.PP
.nf
sudo cti\-agent cti\-audit \-\-check
.fi
.PP
Write one event from a script (what
.BR fleet\-owners (1)
does after a change commits):
.PP
.nf
cti\-audit \-\-lane fleet\-owners \-\-action owners.set \e
    \-\-target id:12345 \-\-outcome success \-\-detail "owner changed"
.fi
.PP
See what the cleanup would delete, then delete it:
.PP
.nf
sudo cti\-agent cti\-audit \-\-prune \-\-days 120 \-\-dry\-run
sudo cti\-agent cti\-audit \-\-prune \-\-days 120
.fi
.PP
Read today's trail, and follow one unit run (the run id is systemd's
invocation id):
.PP
.nf
sudo tail \-f /var/log/cti\-agent/audit\-$(date \-u +%F).log
sudo grep 'run="b3f2' /var/log/cti\-agent/audit\-*.log
.fi
.PP
Ship the trail to a collector: set
.B AUDIT_SYSLOG_ADDR=siem.example.com:6514
in fleet.env, then run the check above.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /var/log/cti\-agent/audit\-YYYY\-MM\-DD.log
The trail.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR run\-mailbox\-cleanup (1),
.BR fleet\-owners (1),
.BR cti\-mailer (1),
.BR cti\-jira (1)
